## This app

- Code is grouped by domain, and a domain is a folder under `app/`. Its types, queries and rules sit next to its pages, with their tests. `app/todos/todo.go` (`package todos`) is the todo list. The home page (`app/route.go`) and the page of one todo (`app/todos/todo+/route.go`) import `{{.Module}}/app/todos`.
- A new domain gets a folder under `app/` and a file named after it, such as `app/notes/note.go` (`package notes`). When it has a list page and a page per item, its code goes in the list folder, not in the `name+` folder of one item.
- The root package (`package {{.Package}}`, at the module root) holds only the hooks (`hooks.go`), the options (`options.go`: `var DBPath`) and what the whole app shares (`db.go`: `var DB`, `schema.sql` with the tables of every domain, and `sessions.go`, the session store that keeps sessions in the database, so a restart signs nobody out). Domains import it as `"{{.Module}}"` and write `{{.Package}}.DB`. By convention it imports nothing under `app/`: Go refuses import cycles, so once a domain uses `{{.Package}}.DB`, the root package could not import that domain or a page that imports it.
- A domain may import another one that does not depend on it: if `notes` imports `todos`, directly or through other packages, `todos` cannot import `notes`. There are no `models/`, `services/` or `handlers/` packages. See `docs/structure.md`.
- This app wires dependencies by hand: package-level variables such as `DB`, set up in `OnStart`, not in `init()`.

